Basic Git Commands
•	git init
o	Description: Starts a brand new, empty Git repository in your current project folder.
o	Sample usage: git init
o	Common options/flags: --bare (creates a repository without a working directory for sharing) [1, 2]
•	git restore
o	Description: Discards changes in your working directory or unstages files.
o	Sample usage: git restore index.html
o	Common options/flags: --staged (unstage a file) [1]
•	git status
o	Description: Shows the current state of your working directory and staging area.
o	Sample usage: git status
o	Common options/flags: -s or --short (gives a compact summary) [1, 2]
•	git branch
o	Description: Lists, creates, or deletes branches.
o	Sample usage: git branch feature-login
o	Common options/flags: -d (deletes a branch), -a (lists both local and remote branches) [1, 2, 3]
•	git add
o	Description: Adds changes from your working directory into the staging area.
o	Sample usage: git add .
o	Common options/flags: -A or --all (stages all changes including deletions) [1, 2, 3]
•	git checkout / git switch
o	Description: Switches between different branches or restores files.
o	Sample usage: git switch main or git checkout main
o	Common options/flags: -b (creates and switches to a new branch) [1, 2, 3]
•	git commit
o	Description: Saves a permanent snapshot of your staged changes into the project history.
o	Sample usage: git commit -m "Fix homepage bug"
o	Common options/flags: -a (automatically stage and commit modified files), --amend (modify the last commit) [1, 2, 3, 4]
•	git merge
o	Description: Combines history and changes from a separate branch into your current branch.
o	Sample usage: git merge feature-login
o	Common options/flags: --no-ff (creates a merge commit even if a fast-forward is possible) [1, 2, 3]
•	git log
o	Description: Displays the chronological commit history of your repository.
o	Sample usage: git log
o	Common options/flags: --oneline (shows each commit on a single line), --graph (shows an ASCII graph of branch history) [1, 2, 3]
•	git tag
o	Description: Adds a permanent marker or label to a specific commit, usually used for releases.
o	Sample usage: git tag v1.0.0
o	Common options/flags: -a (creates an annotated tag with a message) [1]
•	git diff
o	Description: Shows line-by-line differences between files, commits, or trees.
o	Sample usage: git diff
o	Common options/flags: --staged (shows changes between staged area and last commit) [1]
•	git clone
o	Description: Downloads a complete copy of an existing remote repository onto your computer.
o	Sample usage: git clone https://github.com
o	Common options/flags: --depth 1 (clones only the most recent commit to save time/bandwidth) [1, 2, 3, 4]
The Three-Tree Architecture of Git
Git manages your project using three main states or "trees":
1.	The Working Directory: The actual folder on your computer where you see, edit, and create files. Changes here are untracked or modified until you manually move them forward.
2.	The Staging Area (Index): A hidden file (the index) that acts as a holding zone. When you run git add, you take a snapshot of your working files and place them here, prepping them to be saved. [1]
3.	The HEAD (Local Repository): The final permanent storage area. When you run git commit, Git takes the snapshots from the staging area and stores them securely as permanent history in your local .git database.

